Hairy Dragon wrote:
> If I were to start playing rogue-like games, which game is most recommended?

For learning the controls, I always thought I got used to the many
different keypresses used in Nethack so easily because I had played a
good bit of Rogue first.  Rogue is hard, but it serves as a good
introduction if you can get the past the fact that you're unlikely to
win.

As far as more recent roguelikes go, the oft-unstated fact about
Nethack is that it is fair.  As marvin has proven, almost every game is
winnable.  That makes it good for new users to pick up.  What makes it
worse, however, is that there's a huge amount of spoilers to read that
are all but necessary to do well.  On the other hand, that could be
seen as indicative of the game's tremendous depth, and there is nothing
in the world quite like the feeling of reasoning that something should
logically be possible, trying it out, and realizing that IT WAS IN THE
GAME.

Angband I'm slowly warming to, and it's a much simpler game as far as
what you need to know to play, but some spoilers, really, are necessary
there too, just of a different type: what level to hover at to get
stats, what levels not to go past without which resistances, how to
deal with summoners and ultra-dangerous monsters, and things like that.
 It is also harder for a completely-spoiled player.

Good things have been said about Dungeon Crawl, an up-and-comer, but
it's supposed to be quite difficult.

Honestly, I think a bit of Rogue then Nethack is the best way to go, at
this time.

